{"v":"3.0.0","project":{"accession":"PRJNA1503748","title":"Navy bean hydration wastewater from the canning process as a fermentable substrate for the gut microbiota","description":"Thermal hydration of navy bean (Phaseolus vulgaris L.) during canned bean processing generates large volumes of wastewater enriched with bioactive compounds, including soluble fibers (raffinose-family oligosaccharides), saponins, and polyphenols. This wastewater is usually discarded, and its potential to modulate the gut microbiota remains underexplored. This study evaluated the in vitro faecal fermentation of a recovered navy bean wastewater extract (NBWE) against fructo-oligosaccharides (FOS). We assessed gas production, short-chain fatty acid (SCFA) formation, and microbial community dynamics using shotgun metagenomics to profile both bacterial microbiome and viral (bacteriophage) communities. NBWE supported rapid gas production and SCFA formation, particularly acetate, at levels comparable to FOS. Microbial analysis revealed no significant treatment effects on alpha or beta diversity. Instead, microbial communities underwent significant time-dependent restructuring across all treatments, characterised by increased Bacillota and reduced Bacteroidota. Both NBWE and FOS supplementation were associated with a decline in the initially dominant genus Prevotella, alongside enrichment of SCFA-associated genera including Roseburia, Blautia, Lachnospira, and Faecalibacterium. Predicted metabolic pathway analysis identified these taxa as potential contributors to acetate formation. Virome community profiles shifted alongside bacterial community restructuring. Predicted auxiliary metabolic genes linked to carbohydrate and lipid metabolism suggested a potential role for bacteriophages in fermentation-related metabolism, although this prediction requires further experimental validation. Overall, NBWE functioned as a fermentable, value-added substrate that supported acetate production and was associated with gut microbial metabolic modulation.","organism":null,"data_type":"Raw sequence reads","center_name":"Monash University","submission_date":"2026/07/29 00:00","last_update":null},"sample_cols":["accession","title","collection_date","country","region","city","lat","lon","urban_status","urban_context","eligibility_reason","isolation_source","env_broad_scale","env_local_scale","env_medium","host","location_resolution","country_iso2","place_id","location_status","location_method","location_note","geo_loc_raw","collection_year"],"samples":[["SAMN62029996","Sample without fermentation","2024","Australia","Victoria","Melbourne",-37.8136,144.9631,"non_environmental",null,"host-derived or clinical material","stool",null,null,null,"Homo sapiens","coordinate","AU",2158177,"verified","text+coordinates",null,"Australia: Melbourne",2024],["SAMN62029997","Sample without fermentation","2024","Australia","Victoria","Melbourne",-37.8136,144.9631,"non_environmental",null,"host-derived or clinical material","stool",null,null,null,"Homo sapiens","coordinate","AU",2158177,"verified","text+coordinates",null,"Australia: Melbourne",2024],["SAMN62029998","Sample without fermentation","2024","Australia","Victoria","Melbourne",-37.8136,144.9631,"non_environmental",null,"host-derived or clinical material","stool",null,null,null,"Homo sapiens","coordinate","AU",2158177,"verified","text+coordinates",null,"Australia: Melbourne",2024],["SAMN62029999","At 4 hr of fermentation","2024","Australia","Victoria","Melbourne",-37.8136,144.9631,"non_environmental",null,"host-derived or clinical material","stool",null,null,null,"Homo sapiens","coordinate","AU",2158177,"verified","text+coordinates",null,"Australia: Melbourne",2024],["SAMN62030000","At 4 hr of fermentation","2024","Australia","Victoria","Melbourne",-37.8136,144.9631,"non_environmental",null,"host-derived or clinical material","stool",null,null,null,"Homo sapiens","coordinate","AU",2158177,"verified","text+coordinates",null,"Australia: 